  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 101 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nHot and Dry Early Week:\n\nOnce Isaias shifts to the east on Sunday, models show the\naforementioned ridge building over the region, and with it, warm\nair advection pushing 850 mb temperatures into the 18-20 deg C\nrange. Associated surface temperatures in the upper 80s and low\n90s will challenge daily records Sunday through Tuesday. These\ntemperatures are 10-20 degrees above climatological averages. See\nthe Climate section below for more details.\n\n\nA lee-low is progged to strengthen east of the Rockies on Sunday\nin response to a weak shortwave that transits the northern High\nPlains. The tightened surface pressure gradient will result in \nbreezy winds across far western Missouri and eastern Kansas; \nhedging closer to the NBM 75th percentile suggests gusts of 25 \nmph will be common for locations along and west of the Highway \n65 corridor, and an occasional gust to 30 mph is also possible.\nThese breezy winds in combination with the unseasonably hot and\ndry conditions will produce Elevated fire danger on Sunday. It \nshould be noted that the area of strongest winds will not be \ncollocated with the area of lowest RH. More specifically, the \nlowest RHs will likely be observed across portions of the \neastern Ozarks as the southwesterly winds produce \ndrying/downsloping effects on the northeast side of the plateau.\nPlease see the Fire Weather section below for more detailed fuel \ninformation.\n\nShower and Thunderstorm Chances Mid-week:\n\nThe quiet pattern looks to become complicated rather quickly as\nwe proceed into late Tuesday and through mid-week thanks to a\npair of tropical systems in the Pacific and a digging shortwave\ntrough. Global ensembles depict fairly good agreement in\nsweeping the two tropical systems into the southwest flow across\nthe western CONUS, resulting in a stream of rich moisture \ncontent ahead of the upper-level shortwave. Cluster analysis \ndepicts increasingly divergent solutions as this wave shifts \neast through the Plains, but they can roughly be categorized \ninto two groups. The majority of ensembles depict convection \ndeveloping along a cold front as it sags through Missouri late \nTuesday before eventually stalling out and lifting back to the \nnortheast as a warm front when the parent upper- level trough \npivots through the Midwest sometime late Thursday into Friday. \nThis solution would result in a longer period of rain chances \nthat last through Thursday and possibly into Friday. The \nalternative is a more progressive shortwave that pushes through \nthe region more quickly, winding rain chances down Thursday \nmorning. A glance at NAEFS Ensemble Situational Awareness Tables\ndoes indeed reveal a signal for abundant moisture content \nacross southwest Missouri and southeast Kansas should convection\nform. Current NBM rain chances increase Tuesday night (30-60%),\nmax out on Wednesday (50-75%), and decrease Thursday afternoon \n(20-40%). Furthermore, if the strengthening upper-level flow \nbecomes coincident with the deeply moist and unstable airmass, a\nrisk for more organized convection may become apparent. Given \nthe complexity of the forecast, it is still too early to \npinpoint rainfall amounts at this time, and these details will \nbe ironed out in future forecast updates.\n\nIn short, there are a number of factors that all promote rain \nof some sort across southwest Missouri and southeast Kansas mid-\nweek, but it is just a matter of how, if, and when these \nfeatures converge that will determine the sensible weather we\nexperience. \n\nCooler temperatures will move in behind the precipitation\nchances late week, if only briefly.",
